{
  "id": 2,
  "slug": "into-the-middle",
  "title": "Into the Middle",
  "concept": "Why application servers sit in private subnets, and how security groups chain one tier to the next.",
  "guidance": "full",
  "region": "us-east-1",
  "azs": 2,
  "narration": [
    {
      "id": "welcome",
      "text": "Altitude means distance from the internet. The instances sit in the mid band, in private subnets: no kite string from the ground can reach that high. Let us prove it.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "first-run",
      "text": "Press \"Fly kites\". The red kite is aiming straight at an instance this time. Watch where it goes.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "explain",
      "text": "The red kite drifted along the VPC border and found no gate: an instance has only a private address, and nothing on the internet can route to one. But the green kites failed too. The load balancer has no targets yet.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "register",
      "text": "Click the load balancer and tick both instances as targets, one in each Availability Zone.",
      "highlight": "resource:alb",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "targets-registered",
          "loadBalancerId": "alb",
          "atLeast": 2
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "chain",
      "text": "Now look at an instance. Its security group allows HTTP 80 from Anywhere: far wider than it needs. Click an instance, remove that rule, and add HTTP 80 from the security group \"alb-sg\". Now only the load balancer may call.",
      "highlight": "resource:app-a",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "scene",
        "predicate": {
          "kind": "targets-accept-only-load-balancer",
          "loadBalancerId": "alb"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "second-run",
      "text": "Fly the kites again.",
      "highlight": "button:fly",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "run-finished"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wrap",
      "text": "Each tier now trusts only the tier in front of it. Naming a security group instead of an address range is called chaining, and it stays correct however the load balancer's addresses change.",
      "advanceWhen": {
        "kind": "acknowledged"
      }
    }
  ],
